🔁 abstract vs final Class in Java
| Feature              | `abstract` class                            | `final` class                         |
| -------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| **Purpose**          | To provide a base class for inheritance     | To prevent inheritance                |
| **Inheritance**      | Must be inherited                           | Cannot be inherited                   |
| **Object Creation**  | ❌ Cannot create object directly             | ✅ Can create object                   |
| **Abstract Methods** | Can have abstract and non-abstract methods  | ❌ Cannot have abstract methods        |
| **Usage Scenario**   | Common behavior with partial implementation | Security, utility, or immutable class |
| **Keyword**          | `abstract class ClassName {}`               | `final class ClassName {}`            |
| **Example**          | `abstract class Animal {}`                  | `final class MathUtils {}`            |


✅ Example of abstract Class:
java
Copy
Edit
abstract class Animal {
    abstract void sound(); // abstract method
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating...");
    }
}
✅ Example of final Class:
java
Copy
Edit
final class MathUtils {
    static int square(int x) {
        return x * x;
    }
}
🔑 Summary:
Use **abstract** when you want other classes to extend and implement behavior.

Use **final** when you want to prevent extension and keep behavior unchanged.